#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084397 :tutorial/content "A repeating decimal can be expressed as a geometric series whose common ratio is a power of $\\dfrac 1 {10}.$\n\nFor example, the number $0.\\overline{8}$ can be expressed as \n![](images/03/03acd5b86ea1c8f214f08482c17a8631bd8d55754d4e5ddd974fa17a9ac9cb02.svg)\nWe have $a_1 = \\dfrac{8}{10}$ and $r = \\dfrac {0.08}{0.8} = \\dfrac 1 {10}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.\\overset{―}{8} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{10}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{10})}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{10})}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}\\cdot 10 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3998b477-1c28-5edc-8605-2738ce474d94" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1212 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
